ry other type (0.67 with re-ranking; `eval/longmemeval/README.md`). | decided |
 | D-92 | **Recall briefs turns with their neighbours, grouped by conversation in time order** (M6-T13). A ranked hit in a long chat is one message; the answer often sits in the message before or after it, and a 2.6B reader given five whole sessions is distracted by the rest of them. (a) Kernel `recall` (memory profile) adds to each recalled message the messages just before and after it (`as:inReplyTo` either way), and shows these excerpts after the other lines, grouped by conversation (`as:context`), oldest first, each in time order (`as:published`), with `[...]` where messages were skipped. Lines are chosen in rank order within the budget; memories and claims keep their own lines, now with the date when a note has one. (b) Measured on LongMemEval (101 questions, `liquid/lfm-2.5-2.6b:free` reading, Haiku judging blind): 0.683 against 0.624 for the top 5 whole sessions and 0.703 for the oracle, at about the same context size; knowledge update 0.54 to 0.85, preference 0.33 to 0.67, multi-session 0.44 to 0.36 (two questions). (c) `KK_DENSE=0` keeps recall on BM25 with dense embeddings installed, as `KK_RERANK=0` turns re-ranking off, so the two can be compared on one database. | decided |
 | D-93 | **Memory extractor: one message at a time, a small model, quotes checked in code** (M6-T4). (a) A processor (`modules/memory-extractor`, `kk serve` part `extract`, opt-in) reads each recorded message (an as:Note in a conversation) once, oldest first per conversation, after its own earlier change sets are decided, so a correction sees the memory it corrects. The model gets the message, the message before it (to read short answers), and up to 8 earlier memories in its scope (the conversation's latest, then what recall finds), numbered. It answers a JSON array: kind, a sentence that stands alone, a quote, which earlier memory it replaces, a topic, a date ahead, a standing instruction. (b) The memories are the shape `remember` writes (D-88), attributed to the message's author, `dcterms:isPartOf` the message, evidence `extracted`, quoting the message in its transcript; a replaced memory is superseded claim by claim; a date ahead becomes an `on` cue and an always/never instruction an `always` cue (D-91). (c) A small model is unreliable, so code checks what it can: a quote must be in the message (else the message sentence sharing most words with the memory is quoted); a memory sharing no word with the message is dropped (the model repeats memories from other messages); from an agent's message only commitments are kept (the rest of what it said is in the message itself, which recall briefs with its neighbours, D-92); an unknown kind becomes a fact, or a preference for a standing instruction; a cut-off JSON array keeps its complete items. (d) Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (`KK_EXTRACT_URL`, `KK_EXTRACT_MODEL`, default `liquid/lfm-2.5-2.6b:free` on OpenRouter); the smoke test uses a stand-in model. (e) Measured on a fixture of three conversations (`eval/expected.yaml`, 10 expected memories, a correction, an instruction, three messages that should give nothing): 1.0, 1.0 and 0.8 of the expected memories over three runs of the 2.6B model, every correction replacing the right memory, every instruction cued. The checks in (c) were tuned on these runs; LongMemEval is the held-out test. | decided |
+| D-94 | **One briefing, three deliveries** (M6-T7). The memory profile's `recall` briefing (who said it, belief, #handle, messages with their neighbours, D-92) is what an agent gets however it is delivered: (a) the per-prompt hook gives it within 400 tokens, keeping only lines the cross-encoder scores at -2.5 or more (calibrated on a handful of pairs: related prompts scored -2.0 to 0.0, unrelated -3.2 and lower), so a prompt about something else brings nothing; `recall` itself keeps no threshold. Without a cross-encoder, BM25's matches are kept as before. (b) The session-start hook adds what others changed since the agent's last finished session (`changes_since`) after the standing memories. (c) `kk brief --write AGENTS.md` (or CLAUDE.md) writes the standing memories and the last week's changes between markers, for agents with neither MCP nor hooks. The hooks call the MCP tools' own functions, so the three never drift apart. | decided |
 
